{"ideas":[{"id":"idea_01","risks":"This may only certify the already-known finite/no-gap result and still leave the original scalar-tail goal open. Hidden gaps could remain in the policy class, sign convention, or grid-to-continuum comparison.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","assumption audit"],"summary":"Turn the solver's finite-class/product-closed characterization into a referee-style theorem package: finite grid identity, positive-part continuum approximation with G_T, product-closed infinite-class extension, and the exact public-exogenous randomized policy assumptions. Check every quantifier and whether the solver overclaims beyond these hypotheses.","title":"Gate the Bayes Iff","why_promising":"This is the closest thing to a necessary-and-sufficient theorem already in the notes; a precise audit can determine whether it is a valid partial solution or still has a fatal gap."},{"id":"idea_02","risks":"The implementation may preserve only reward coordinates, not behavior; if the original policy model allows law-by-law versions, private feedback, or action effects, the theorem may fail or need stronger domination.","suggested_lenses":["measure theory","proof strategy"],"summary":"Finish the original Borel-policy no-gap theorem under payoff-weighted reward-measure domination. Prove or cite the completed-measurable-to-Borel version lemma, stochastic-kernel randomization lemma, weak-star closed simplex constraints, and ensure the same policy class is used for finite subclasses and the full class.","title":"Close Borel No-Gap","why_promising":"The completed L-infinity kernel theorem is essentially accepted; closing this bridge would make the infinite-class no-gap result much more theorem-ready."},{"id":"idea_03","risks":"Weak/Feller continuity is already known to be insufficient, and the search may produce only pathological non-Borel gaps or overly strong TV assumptions.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","counterexample search"],"summary":"Search for natural infinite-class no-gap hypotheses beyond domination: identifiable Borel parameter classes, TV-approximable reward measures with constants, compact statistical experiments, or Borel nondominated non-identifiable counterexamples where finite-subclass Bayes gaps miss full-class regret.","title":"Natural Infinite Classes","why_promising":"The solver's characterization becomes much more useful if product closedness can be verified under natural statistical assumptions, or if a natural obstruction shows why it cannot."},{"id":"idea_04","risks":"The desired comparator-specific delay theorem may be false; a two-arm proof may not extend to many arms, and lower bounds may depend sensitively on feedback timing.","suggested_lenses":["algorithm design","adversarial lower bound"],"summary":"Attack the comparator-specific heterogeneous-delay experts conjecture. Start with the two-arm delayed-advantage formulation and try to combine the accepted solvency invariant with a reserve or wealth potential proving slow capture; alternatively construct a sequence refuting sqrt(T d_i log N)-type comparator-delay regret.","title":"Heterogeneous Delays","why_promising":"A positive result would immediately give the conditional tail-adaptive truncation bound min_H {delta_T(H)+B_T(H)}, improving the fixed-H upper theorem."},{"id":"idea_05","risks":"The periodic two-window obstruction may rule out many plausible nested-window guarantees; continuum quotes or compromise quotes could complicate the finite-grid obstruction.","suggested_lenses":["algorithm design","counterexample search"],"summary":"After the strong simultaneous nested-window oracle was refuted, look for weaker adaptive-truncation guarantees: true min-over-window bounds, different charges than V_M - V_m, randomized model-selection over windows, or continuum/dense-grid variants of the periodic obstruction.","title":"Weaker Adaptive Truncation","why_promising":"This may recover adaptivity without proving the full heterogeneous-delay experts conjecture, and the existing obstruction gives a concrete stress test for any proposal."},{"id":"idea_06","risks":"Persistent quotes may corrupt intended tail envelopes; independent hidden bits can collapse against a single fixed comparator; information-tail tradeoffs may make matching rates impossible.","suggested_lenses":["information theory","counterexample search"],"summary":"Use the finite-grid Psi value as a design laboratory for matching power-tail lower bounds. Test noisy common-parameter, multi-hypothesis codebook, and limited-leakage constructions by first computing their Bayes gap and then checking whether they can be realized as market price processes with controlled tail/truncation profiles.","title":"Codebook Tail Lower Bounds","why_promising":"Matching lower rates remain the largest open hole; the Psi diagnostic can reject bad constructions before spending effort on market realization."},{"id":"idea_07","risks":"Hidden/labeled examples rule out any per-law scalar iff, so this may only yield better sufficient conditions or notation, not a characterization.","suggested_lenses":["algebraic proof","counterexample search"],"summary":"Develop the last-exceedance identity into value-tail formulas for V_T, delta_T(H), and Delta_T(H). Look for value-weighted exceedance-age conditions that strictly improve beta-style sufficient bounds and explain rare-history/tiny-price examples cleanly.","title":"Last-Exceedance Calculus","why_promising":"The fixed-quote reward is exactly controlled by last future exceedances, so this may identify the right value-weighted tail quantities for upper bounds."},{"id":"idea_08","risks":"A doubling-style or randomized horizon schedule may defeat the simple two-deadline obstruction; the exact comparator convention across horizons must be fixed carefully.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","counterexample search"],"summary":"Formalize the anytime known-law boundary. Turn the super-growing two-deadline deterministic sequence into a rigorous lower bound, or show an anytime law-aware policy can still get sublinear regret for every horizon.","title":"Anytime Known Law","why_promising":"The known-law oracle is horizon-aware. Settling the anytime variant would sharpen the model taxonomy and prevent overreading the oracle lemma."},{"id":"idea_09","risks":"This may be outside the intended protocol; action-dependent markets require many possible causal comparator definitions and may become a different problem.","suggested_lenses":["model audit","counterexample search"],"summary":"Audit the action-dependent-price regime. Specify a causal protocol and comparator semantics, then test whether the delayed full-information truncation proof survives or whether simple action-dependent examples break it.","title":"Action-Dependent Boundary","why_promising":"The solver's theorem package assumes public exogenous prices. If the original problem permits action-dependent prices, this is a major missing case."},{"id":"idea_10","risks":"A broad impossibility statement about all scalar profiles can become too meta unless the allowed profile data is defined precisely.","suggested_lenses":["theorem formulation","counterexample search"],"summary":"Generalize the hidden/labeled q-terminal pair into a clean impossibility theorem: no criterion depending only on individual laws' reward, beta, Delta, delta, comparator, or truncation profiles and their envelopes can characterize unknown-class minimax learnability.","title":"Scalar Tail No-Go","why_promising":"This directly addresses the original request for a tail characterization by delineating what kind of tail data is provably insufficient."}],"solution_status":"maybe"}
